**Pitfall:** Swapping the loops (amounts outside, coins inside) counts ordered sequences, so 1+2 and 2+1 are counted separately (that's Combination Sum IV).

**Key idea:** The running sum after `i` numbers lies in `[−total, total]`, so `(index, running sum)` has few distinct states. Even better: if `P` is the plus-group sum, `P − (total − P) = target`, so count subsets summing to `(total + target) / 2`.

**Brute force:** Try all 2ⁿ sign patterns. Time O(2ⁿ), Space O(n) recursion. (Fine for n ≤ 20, but the DPs are much faster.)

:::details 1. Top-down DP on (index, running sum)
`count(i, s)` = number of ways to finish from index `i` with current sum `s`: `count(i+1, s + nums[i]) + count(i+1, s − nums[i])`, and at the end 1 if `s == target`.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private int[] nums;
    private int target;
    private Map<String, Integer> memo = new HashMap<>();

    public int findTargetSumWays(int[] nums, int target) {
        this.nums = nums;
        this.target = target;
        return count(0, 0);
    }

    private int count(int i, int sum) {
        if (i == nums.length) return sum == target ? 1 : 0;
        String key = i + "," + sum;
        Integer cached = memo.get(key);
        if (cached != null) return cached;
        int res = count(i + 1, sum + nums[i]) + count(i + 1, sum - nums[i]);
        memo.put(key, res);
        return res;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
