**Time:** O(n³)
**Space:** O(n²)
:::

**Pitfall:** Choosing the balloon burst *first* in a range. Its neighbours then depend on bursts outside the subproblem, so the halves aren't independent.

**Key idea:** `match(i, j)` = does `s[i:]` match `p[j:]`. If `p[j+1]` is `*`, either skip `x*` entirely (`match(i, j+2)`) or, when `s[i]` matches `x`, consume one char and stay on the star (`match(i+1, j)`). Otherwise the current chars must match and both advance.

**Brute force:** The same recursion without caching. Stars make it branch at every character. Time O(2^(n+m)), Space O(n + m).

:::details 1. Top-down DP (memoized recursion)
Base case: when the pattern is used up, succeed only if the string is too. `first = i < n and p[j] in {s[i], '.'}`. Cache on `(i, j)`.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private String s, p;
    private Boolean[][] memo;

    public boolean isMatch(String s, String p) {
        this.s = s;
        this.p = p;
        memo = new Boolean[s.length() + 1][p.length() + 1];
        return match(0, 0);
    }

    private boolean match(int i, int j) {
        if (j == p.length()) return i == s.length();
        if (memo[i][j] != null) return memo[i][j];
        boolean first = i < s.length() && (p.charAt(j) == '.' || p.charAt(j) == s.charAt(i));
        boolean res;
        if (j + 1 < p.length() && p.charAt(j + 1) == '*')
            res = match(i, j + 2) || (first && match(i + 1, j)); // skip x*, or use it once more
        else
            res = first && match(i + 1, j + 1);
        return memo[i][j] = res;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def isMatch(self, s: str, p: str) -> bool:
        @cache
        def match(i: int, j: int) -> bool:
            if j == len(p):
                return i == len(s)
            first = i < len(s) and p[j] in (s[i], ".")
            if j + 1 < len(p) and p[j + 1] == "*":
                return match(i, j + 2) or (first and match(i + 1, j))  # skip x*, or use it once more
            return first and match(i + 1, j + 1)

        return match(0, 0)
```

**Time:** O(n·m)
**Space:** O(n·m)
:::

:::details 2. Bottom-up table
`dp[i][j]` for every suffix pair, filled from `i = n` down and `j = m − 1` down, with `dp[n][m] = true`. Row `i = n` (empty string) is still needed: patterns like `a*b*` match the empty string.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isMatch(String s, String p) {
        int n = s.length(), m = p.length();
        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[n + 1][m + 1];
        dp[n][m] = true;
        for (int i = n; i >= 0; i--)
            for (int j = m - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
                boolean first = i < n && (p.charAt(j) == '.' || p.charAt(j) == s.charAt(i));
                if (j + 1 < m && p.charAt(j + 1) == '*')
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i][j + 2] || (first && dp[i + 1][j]);
                else
                    dp[i][j] = first && dp[i + 1][j + 1];
            }
        return dp[0][0];
    }
}
```

