                end = farthest
        return jumps
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(1)
:::

:::details 2. Bottom-up DP
`dp[i]` = fewest jumps from `i` to the end, `dp[n−1] = 0`. From right to left, `dp[i] = 1 + min(dp[j])` over the indices `j` you can land on.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int jump(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int[] dp = new int[n];
        for (int i = n - 2; i >= 0; i--) {
            dp[i] = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
            for (int j = i + 1; j <= Math.min(n - 1, i + nums[i]); j++)
                if (dp[j] != Integer.MAX_VALUE) dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], dp[j] + 1);
        }
        return dp[0];
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
