**Time:** O(n · max jump), O(n²) worst case
**Space:** O(n)
:::

**Pitfall:** Looping `i` all the way to `n − 1` adds a jump when you're already standing on the last index. Stop at `n − 2`.

**Key idea:** If total gas ≥ total cost, a valid start exists. If the tank goes negative while driving from `start` to `i`, no station between `start` and `i` can be the start either, so the next candidate is `i + 1`.

**Brute force:** Simulate a full lap from every station. Time O(n²), Space O(1).

:::details 1. One-pass greedy (optimal)
Keep `total` (sum of all `gas − cost`) and `tank` (fuel since the current candidate). When `tank < 0`, reset it and move the candidate to `i + 1`. Return the candidate if `total ≥ 0`, else −1.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int canCompleteCircuit(int[] gas, int[] cost) {
        int total = 0, tank = 0, start = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < gas.length; i++) {
            int diff = gas[i] - cost[i];
            total += diff;
            tank += diff;
            if (tank < 0) { // can't get past i from start: try i + 1
                tank = 0;
                start = i + 1;
            }
        }
        return total >= 0 ? start : -1;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def canCompleteCircuit(self, gas: List[int], cost: List[int]) -> int:
        total = tank = start = 0
        for i, (g, c) in enumerate(zip(gas, cost)):
            total += g - c
            tank += g - c
            if tank < 0:  # can't get past i from start: try i + 1
                tank, start = 0, i + 1
        return start if total >= 0 else -1
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(1)
:::

:::details 2. Two pointers growing the route from both ends
Treat the route as a window from `start` (moving left from the end) to `end` (moving right from 0). If the tank is negative, extend the start one station earlier (adding its surplus); otherwise extend the end forward. When the two meet, the window covers the whole circle.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int canCompleteCircuit(int[] gas, int[] cost) {
        int n = gas.length, start = n - 1, end = 0;
        int tank = gas[start] - cost[start];
        while (start > end) {
            if (tank < 0) { // borrow surplus from an earlier start
                start--;
                tank += gas[start] - cost[start];
            } else { // drive one station further
                tank += gas[end] - cost[end];
                end++;
            }
        }
        return tank >= 0 ? start : -1;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def canCompleteCircuit(self, gas: List[int], cost: List[int]) -> int:
        start, end = len(gas) - 1, 0
        tank = gas[start] - cost[start]
        while start > end:
            if tank < 0:  # borrow surplus from an earlier start
                start -= 1
                tank += gas[start] - cost[start]
            else:  # drive one station further
                tank += gas[end] - cost[end]
                end += 1
        return start if tank >= 0 else -1
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(1)
:::

**Pitfall:** After a reset, the candidate is `i + 1`, not `start + 1`. Every station between them would fail at the same point.

**Key idea:** The smallest remaining card must start a group (nothing smaller is left to precede it). Repeatedly take the smallest value `x` and consume one each of `x, x+1, …, x+groupSize−1`.

**Brute force:** Sort, then for each group scan the array for each needed value and remove it from a list. Time O(n²), Space O(n).

:::details 1. Sorted counts (TreeMap / sorted keys)
Count every value. Walk the distinct values in increasing order; if value `x` still has count `c > 0`, it must start `c` groups, so subtract `c` from `x … x+groupSize−1`. Any count going negative means failure.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isNStraightHand(int[] hand, int groupSize) {
        if (hand.length % groupSize != 0) return false;
        TreeMap<Integer, Integer> count = new TreeMap<>();
        for (int x : hand) count.merge(x, 1, Integer::sum);
        for (int x : count.keySet()) {
            int c = count.get(x);
            if (c == 0) continue;
            for (int v = x; v < x + groupSize; v++) { // x starts c groups
                int have = count.getOrDefault(v, 0);
                if (have < c) return false;
                count.put(v, have - c);
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def isNStraightHand(self, hand: List[int], groupSize: int) -> bool:
        if len(hand) % groupSize:
            return False
        count = Counter(hand)
        for x in sorted(count):
            c = count[x]
            if c == 0:
                continue
            for v in range(x, x + groupSize):  # x starts c groups
                if count[v] < c:
                    return False
                count[v] -= c
        return True
```

**Time:** O(n log n) to sort the distinct values
**Space:** O(n)
:::

:::details 2. Min-heap
Push the distinct values into a min-heap. The top is the smallest open value; take one of each of the next `groupSize` values from the count map. When a value's count hits 0 it must be the heap top (otherwise a smaller value is still waiting and can never be completed), so pop it.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isNStraightHand(int[] hand, int groupSize) {
        if (hand.length % groupSize != 0) return false;
        Map<Integer, Integer> count = new HashMap<>();
        for (int x : hand) count.merge(x, 1, Integer::sum);
        PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(count.keySet());
        while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
            int first = heap.peek();
            for (int v = first; v < first + groupSize; v++) {
                Integer have = count.get(v);
                if (have == null || have == 0) return false;
                count.put(v, have - 1);
                if (have - 1 == 0) {
                    if (v != heap.peek()) return false; // a smaller value is left unfinished
                    heap.poll();
                }
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def isNStraightHand(self, hand: List[int], groupSize: int) -> bool:
        if len(hand) % groupSize:
            return False
        count = Counter(hand)
        heap = list(count)
        heapq.heapify(heap)
        while heap:
            first = heap[0]
            for v in range(first, first + groupSize):
                if count[v] == 0:
                    return False
                count[v] -= 1
                if count[v] == 0:
                    if v != heap[0]:
                        return False  # a smaller value is left unfinished
                    heapq.heappop(heap)
        return True
```

**Time:** O(n log n)
**Space:** O(n)
:::

:::details 3. Find group starts without sorting
For any card `x`, walk down while `x − 1` is still in the count map; that's the start of a run. From that start, keep forming groups while the start's count is positive, then move on. Each value is consumed once, so no sort is needed.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isNStraightHand(int[] hand, int groupSize) {
        if (hand.length % groupSize != 0) return false;
        Map<Integer, Integer> count = new HashMap<>();
        for (int x : hand) count.merge(x, 1, Integer::sum);
        for (int x : hand) {
            int start = x;
            while (count.getOrDefault(start - 1, 0) > 0) start--; // walk down to the run's start
            while (start <= x) {
                while (count.getOrDefault(start, 0) > 0) {
                    for (int v = start; v < start + groupSize; v++) {
                        int have = count.getOrDefault(v, 0);
                        if (have == 0) return false;
                        count.put(v, have - 1);
                    }
                }
                start++;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def isNStraightHand(self, hand: List[int], groupSize: int) -> bool:
        if len(hand) % groupSize:
            return False
        count = Counter(hand)
        for x in hand:
            start = x
            while count[start - 1] > 0:
                start -= 1  # walk down to the run's start
            while start <= x:
                while count[start] > 0:
                    for v in range(start, start + groupSize):
                        if count[v] == 0:
                            return False
                        count[v] -= 1
                start += 1
        return True
```

**Time:** O(n · groupSize) in the worst case, about O(n) in practice
**Space:** O(n)
:::

**Pitfall:** Forgetting the quick check `hand.length % groupSize != 0 → false`, and starting groups at an arbitrary card instead of the smallest one.

**Key idea:** Max can only grow values, so any triplet with a value **above** the target in some position is poison and must never be used. Among the safe triplets, check that each target position is matched exactly by at least one of them.

**Brute force:** Try every subset of triplets and check whether its element-wise max equals the target. Time O(2ⁿ), Space O(n).

:::details 1. Filter and mark matched positions
Skip triplets that exceed the target anywhere. For each remaining one, mark position `k` as done if it equals `target[k]`. Return true when all three positions are marked.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean mergeTriplets(int[][] triplets, int[] target) {
        boolean[] done = new boolean[3];
        for (int[] t : triplets) {
            if (t[0] > target[0] || t[1] > target[1] || t[2] > target[2]) continue; // would overshoot
            for (int k = 0; k < 3; k++)
                if (t[k] == target[k]) done[k] = true;
        }
        return done[0] && done[1] && done[2];
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def mergeTriplets(self, triplets: List[List[int]], target: List[int]) -> bool:
        done = set()
        for t in triplets:
            if any(t[k] > target[k] for k in range(3)):
                continue  # would overshoot
            done.update(k for k in range(3) if t[k] == target[k])
        return len(done) == 3
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(1)
:::

:::details 2. Merge all safe triplets
Merging more safe triplets never hurts, since none can push a value past the target. So take the element-wise max of every safe triplet and compare the result with the target.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean mergeTriplets(int[][] triplets, int[] target) {
        int[] merged = new int[3];
        for (int[] t : triplets)
            if (t[0] <= target[0] && t[1] <= target[1] && t[2] <= target[2])
                for (int k = 0; k < 3; k++) merged[k] = Math.max(merged[k], t[k]);
        return Arrays.equals(merged, target);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def mergeTriplets(self, triplets: List[List[int]], target: List[int]) -> bool:
        merged = [0, 0, 0]
        for t in triplets:
            if all(t[k] <= target[k] for k in range(3)):
                merged = [max(m, v) for m, v in zip(merged, t)]
        return merged == target
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(1)
:::

**Pitfall:** Merging a triplet just because one of its values matches the target. If another of its values is too big, the merge overshoots and can't be undone.

**Key idea:** A piece that contains letter `c` must reach at least `c`'s last occurrence. Scan left to right stretching the current piece's end to the last occurrence of each letter seen; when `i` reaches that end, cut.

**Brute force:** For each candidate cut point, check that no letter appears on both sides. Time O(n²), Space O(1).

:::details 1. Last occurrence + greedy cut (optimal)
Record `last[c]`. Walk `i`, with `end = max(end, last[s[i]])`. When `i == end`, the piece `[start, end]` is closed: record its length and start the next piece.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<Integer> partitionLabels(String s) {
        int[] last = new int[26];
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) last[s.charAt(i) - 'a'] = i;
        List<Integer> sizes = new ArrayList<>();
        int start = 0, end = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            end = Math.max(end, last[s.charAt(i) - 'a']);
            if (i == end) { // every letter so far ends inside [start, end]
                sizes.add(end - start + 1);
                start = i + 1;
            }
        }
        return sizes;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def partitionLabels(self, s: str) -> List[int]:
        last = {c: i for i, c in enumerate(s)}
        sizes, start, end = [], 0, 0
        for i, c in enumerate(s):
            end = max(end, last[c])
            if i == end:  # every letter so far ends inside [start, end]
                sizes.append(end - start + 1)
                start = i + 1
        return sizes
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(1) (26 letters)
:::

:::details 2. Merge letter intervals
Each letter spans an interval `[first, last]`. Overlapping intervals must share a piece, so merge them (they're naturally ordered by first occurrence) and output the merged lengths. Same answer, framed as Merge Intervals.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<Integer> partitionLabels(String s) {
        int[] first = new int[26], last = new int[26];
        Arrays.fill(first, -1);
        List<Integer> order = new ArrayList<>(); // letters by first occurrence
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            int c = s.charAt(i) - 'a';
            if (first[c] == -1) { first[c] = i; order.add(c); }
            last[c] = i;
        }
        List<Integer> sizes = new ArrayList<>();
        int curStart = first[order.get(0)], curEnd = last[order.get(0)];
        for (int c : order) {
            if (first[c] > curEnd) { // no overlap: close the merged interval
                sizes.add(curEnd - curStart + 1);
                curStart = first[c];
            }
            curEnd = Math.max(curEnd, last[c]);
        }
        sizes.add(curEnd - curStart + 1);
        return sizes;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def partitionLabels(self, s: str) -> List[int]:
        spans = {}
        for i, c in enumerate(s):
            spans.setdefault(c, [i, i])[1] = i  # letters stay in first-occurrence order
        sizes = []
        cur_start, cur_end = next(iter(spans.values()))
        for first, last in spans.values():
            if first > cur_end:  # no overlap: close the merged interval
                sizes.append(cur_end - cur_start + 1)
                cur_start = first
            cur_end = max(cur_end, last)
        sizes.append(cur_end - cur_start + 1)
        return sizes
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(1)
:::

**Pitfall:** Cutting as soon as the current letter's last occurrence is reached, forgetting that earlier letters in the piece may extend further. Always keep the running `max`.

**Key idea:** You don't need to decide what each star is. Track the **range** `[lo, hi]` of possible open-bracket counts: `(` raises both, `)` lowers both, `*` lowers `lo` and raises `hi`. Fail if `hi < 0`; succeed if `lo` can be 0 at the end.

**Brute force:** Try all 3ᵏ replacements of the k stars and test each string. Time O(3ⁿ·n), Space O(n).

:::details 1. Greedy range of open counts (optimal)
`lo` = fewest possible unmatched `(`, `hi` = most possible. After each char, clamp `lo` at 0 (a negative count is impossible, so those choices are discarded). If `hi` drops below 0, too many `)` have appeared. At the end, return `lo == 0`.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean checkValidString(String s) {
        int lo = 0, hi = 0; // range of possible unmatched '(' counts
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (c == '(') { lo++; hi++; }
            else if (c == ')') { lo--; hi--; }
            else { lo--; hi++; } // '*' could be ')', '(' or empty
            if (hi < 0) return false;
            lo = Math.max(lo, 0);
        }
        return lo == 0;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def checkValidString(self, s: str) -> bool:
        lo = hi = 0  # range of possible unmatched '(' counts
        for c in s:
            if c == "(":
                lo, hi = lo + 1, hi + 1
            elif c == ")":
                lo, hi = lo - 1, hi - 1
            else:
                lo, hi = lo - 1, hi + 1  # '*' could be ')', '(' or empty
            if hi < 0:
                return False
            lo = max(lo, 0)
        return lo == 0
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(1)
:::

:::details 2. Two stacks of indices
Push indices of `(` and `*` onto separate stacks. A `)` matches an open bracket if possible, else a star. At the end, pair each leftover `(` with a star that comes **after** it (compare indices); any `(` left unpaired means false.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean checkValidString(String s) {
        Deque<Integer> open = new ArrayDeque<>(), stars = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            char c = s.charAt(i);
            if (c == '(') open.push(i);
            else if (c == '*') stars.push(i);
            else if (!open.isEmpty()) open.pop();
            else if (!stars.isEmpty()) stars.pop();
            else return false;
        }
        while (!open.isEmpty()) {
            if (stars.isEmpty() || stars.peek() < open.peek()) return false; // star must come after the '('
            open.pop();
            stars.pop();
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def checkValidString(self, s: str) -> bool:
        open_, stars = [], []
        for i, c in enumerate(s):
            if c == "(":
                open_.append(i)
            elif c == "*":
                stars.append(i)
            elif open_:
                open_.pop()
            elif stars:
                stars.pop()
            else:
                return False
        while open_:
            if not stars or stars[-1] < open_[-1]:
                return False  # star must come after the '('
            open_.pop()
            stars.pop()
        return True
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(n)
:::

:::details 3. Top-down DP on (index, open count)
`ok(i, open)`: `(` → `ok(i+1, open+1)`; `)` → `ok(i+1, open−1)` if `open > 0`; `*` → any of the three. At the end require `open == 0`. Cache on `(i, open)`.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private String s;
    private Boolean[][] memo;

    public boolean checkValidString(String s) {
        this.s = s;
        memo = new Boolean[s.length() + 1][s.length() + 1];
        return ok(0, 0);
    }

    private boolean ok(int i, int open) {
        if (open < 0 || open > s.length() - i) return false; // can't close that many
        if (i == s.length()) return open == 0;
        if (memo[i][open] != null) return memo[i][open];
        char c = s.charAt(i);
        boolean res;
        if (c == '(') res = ok(i + 1, open + 1);
        else if (c == ')') res = ok(i + 1, open - 1);
        else res = ok(i + 1, open + 1) || ok(i + 1, open - 1) || ok(i + 1, open);
        return memo[i][open] = res;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def checkValidString(self, s: str) -> bool:
        @cache
        def ok(i: int, open_: int) -> bool:
            if open_ < 0 or open_ > len(s) - i:
                return False  # can't close that many
            if i == len(s):
                return open_ == 0
            c = s[i]
            if c == "(":
                return ok(i + 1, open_ + 1)
            if c == ")":
                return ok(i + 1, open_ - 1)
            return ok(i + 1, open_ + 1) or ok(i + 1, open_ - 1) or ok(i + 1, open_)

        return ok(0, 0)
```

**Time:** O(n²)
**Space:** O(n²)
:::

**Pitfall:** Counting stars as wildcards without positions. In `"*("` the star comes before the `(`, so it can't close it; the stack version must compare indices.

**Key idea:** The list is already sorted, so it splits into three runs: intervals ending before the new one starts, intervals overlapping it (merge them all into one), and intervals starting after it ends.

**Brute force:** Append the new interval, sort everything again and run the standard merge. Time O(n log n), Space O(n).

:::details 1. Linear scan in three phases (optimal)
Copy intervals with `end < new.start`. While `start ≤ new.end`, widen the new interval to `[min starts, max ends]`. Add it, then copy the rest.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int[][] insert(int[][] intervals, int[] newInterval) {
        List<int[]> out = new ArrayList<>();
        int i = 0, n = intervals.length;
        int start = newInterval[0], end = newInterval[1];
        while (i < n && intervals[i][1] < start) out.add(intervals[i++]); // entirely before
        while (i < n && intervals[i][0] <= end) {                          // overlapping: absorb
            start = Math.min(start, intervals[i][0]);
            end = Math.max(end, intervals[i][1]);
            i++;
        }
        out.add(new int[]{start, end});
        while (i < n) out.add(intervals[i++]);                             // entirely after
        return out.toArray(new int[0][]);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def insert(self, intervals: List[List[int]], newInterval: List[int]) -> List[List[int]]:
        out, i, n = [], 0, len(intervals)
        start, end = newInterval
        while i < n and intervals[i][1] < start:  # entirely before
            out.append(intervals[i])
            i += 1
        while i < n and intervals[i][0] <= end:  # overlapping: absorb
            start = min(start, intervals[i][0])
            end = max(end, intervals[i][1])
            i += 1
        out.append([start, end])
        out.extend(intervals[i:])  # entirely after
        return out
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(n) for the output
:::

:::details 2. Binary search for the insert position, then merge
Binary-search where `newInterval` belongs by start, insert it there, then do one merge pass over the list (it's still sorted). The search is O(log n), but building the output is still linear.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int[][] insert(int[][] intervals, int[] newInterval) {
        int lo = 0, hi = intervals.length; // first index whose start > newInterval start
        while (lo < hi) {
            int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
            if (intervals[mid][0] <= newInterval[0]) lo = mid + 1;
            else hi = mid;
        }
        List<int[]> all = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(intervals));
        all.add(lo, newInterval);
        List<int[]> out = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int[] cur : all) {
            if (!out.isEmpty() && out.get(out.size() - 1)[1] >= cur[0])
                out.get(out.size() - 1)[1] = Math.max(out.get(out.size() - 1)[1], cur[1]);
            else
                out.add(new int[]{cur[0], cur[1]});
        }
        return out.toArray(new int[0][]);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
