:::

:::details 2. Greedy, sort by start
Sort by start. When the current interval overlaps the previous kept one, delete whichever ends later, which means keeping `prevEnd = min(prevEnd, end)`. Same answer, framed the other way round.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int eraseOverlapIntervals(int[][] intervals) {
        Arrays.sort(intervals, (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]));
        int removed = 0, prevEnd = intervals[0][1];
        for (int i = 1; i < intervals.length; i++) {
            if (intervals[i][0] >= prevEnd) {
                prevEnd = intervals[i][1];
            } else {
                removed++;
                prevEnd = Math.min(prevEnd, intervals[i][1]); // drop the one ending later
            }
        }
        return removed;
    }
}
```

**Python**

