                heapq.heappop(ends)  # reuse the room that frees first
            heapq.heappush(ends, end)
        return len(ends)
```

**Time:** O(n log n)
**Space:** O(n)
:::

:::details 2. Two sorted arrays (two pointers)
Sort starts and ends separately. Walk the starts: if the next start is before the earliest unprocessed end, you need a new room; otherwise one meeting has ended, so advance the end pointer and reuse its room.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int minMeetingRooms(int[][] intervals) {
        int n = intervals.length;
        int[] starts = new int[n], ends = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { starts[i] = intervals[i][0]; ends[i] = intervals[i][1]; }
        Arrays.sort(starts);
        Arrays.sort(ends);
        int rooms = 0, e = 0;
        for (int s = 0; s < n; s++) {
            if (starts[s] < ends[e]) rooms++; // nobody has left yet: new room
            else e++;                         // a meeting ended: take its room
        }
        return rooms;
    }
}
```

